¶
'''astyllen†''' (as-til′en), ''n.'' [E. dial.; etym. obscure.] A small temporary dam or partition, made either of branches or twigs interlaced, or perhaps sometimes of a simple piece of board, and used either to check the flow of water under ground or to separate ore from refuse or attle on the surface. [Eng.]

'''asunder''' (ạ-sun′dėr), ''prep. phr.'' as ''adv.'' [< ME. ''a sundir'', ''o sunder'', ''on sunder'', etc., < AS. ''on sundran'', apart: see ''a''<sup>3</sup> and ''sunder''.] '''1.''' In or into a position apart; apart or separate, either in position or in direction: said of two or more things: as, wide as the poles ''asunder''.
{{smaller block|The vanguard and rear-guard were above half a league ''asunder'', with the cavalgada between them.
{{right|''Irving'', Granada, p. 78.}}}}
'''2.''' In or into a divided state; into separate parts; in pieces: as, to tear, rend, break, burst, or cut ''asunder''.
{{smaller block|The Lord . . . hath cut ''asunder'' the cords of the wicked.
{{right|Ps. cxxix. 4.}}
What a plaguing thing it is to have a man’s mind torn ''asunder'' by two projects of equal strength.
{{right|''Sterne'', Tristram Shandy, iv. 31.}}
Ties the strongest, influences the sweetest, seem falling ''asunder'' as smoking flax. ''R. Choate'', Addresses, p. 405.}}
'''3.''' Separately; apart. [Archaic.]
{{smaller block|It was impossible to know them ''asunder''.
{{right|''Defoe'', Plague, p. 264.}}}}

